#!/bin/rc

if(~ $#user 0)
	user=`{cat /dev/user}
home=/usr/$user
if(test -f $home/lib/profile){
	echo user directories already made
	exit no
}
cd $home
mkdir -p bin/rc bin/$cputype lib/hotkey tmp
bind -c $home/tmp /tmp

cat > lib/profile <<'!'
bind -a $home/bin/rc /bin
bind -a $home/bin/$cputype /bin
bind -c $home/tmp /tmp
test -e $home/lib/pkg && pkg add -f $home/lib/pkg
test -e $home/lib/factotum && cat $home/lib/factotum >/mnt/factotum/ctl
# $auth, the machine this page came from, serves its / to import(4): its /net, for /net/tcp, and
# webfs, and to its accounts /adm and /mnt/keys. A passkey signs you in (factotum(4)'s webauthn):
#	import ws!$auth!9p / /n/$auth; bind -a /n/$auth/net /net
EDITOR=E
chessa=leela
fn cd { builtin cd $* && awd }
switch($service){
case terminal
	# sound is shared: mixfs binds over /dev/audio, so a second play mixes with the first
	if(test -e /dev/audio && ! grep -s '^mix ' /dev/volume >/dev/null)
		audio/mixfs
	plumber
	dropped &
	prompt=('% ' '	')
	fn %{ $* }
	exec hotkey rio -i riostart
}
!
if(! test -e lib/plumbing)
	cat > lib/plumbing <<!
# to update: cp /usr/$user/lib/plumbing /mnt/plumb/rules

editor = acme

include basic

!
if(! test -e lib/pkg)
	cat > lib/pkg <<!
# the packages the profile adds (pkg(1)): a name, a name and a hash, or a path, a line each
git9
!
if(! test -e lib/hotkey/acme)
	cat > lib/hotkey/acme <<!
# to update: cp /usr/$user/lib/hotkey/acme /mnt/hotkey/rules

include acme.emacs

!
if(! test -e intro.md)
	cat > intro.md <<'!'
# Plan 9 in a Browser

*This file is an introduction to plan9web, a Plan 9 system that runs in a
browser tab. It explains the mouse, the windows and the commands, then
describes what the system adds for its new home: files carried in and out of
the browser, packages, other machines, language servers, drawn windows, and
tools for writing and for programs.*

## Introduction

The window you are reading is acme(1), the Plan 9 text editor, inside rio(1),
the window system. Both are Plan 9 C programs compiled to WebAssembly. They run
on p9web, a Plan 9 kernel written in TypeScript, here in this tab. Each command
you give runs in your browser, and the files you keep in this directory stay in
the storage the browser keeps for this site. Clearing the data of the site in
the browser clears them too.

The system and its ideas come from Plan 9. The best way into them is intro(1),
then the papers in /sys/doc:

	/sys/doc/9.ps            Plan 9 from Bell Labs
	/sys/doc/names.ps        The Use of Name Spaces in Plan 9
	/sys/doc/acme/acme.pdf   Acme: A User Interface for Programmers
	/sys/doc/rc.ps           Rc — The Plan 9 Shell
	/sys/doc/plumb.ps        Plumbing and Other Utilities

To read one, click its name with button 3; on a Mac, Command-click it. For
Acme itself, A Tour of Acme by Russ Cox, a short video, is an excellent
introduction:

	https://research.swtch.com/acme

Button 3 on the address opens it in a new tab. The sections below need only
this file, and name the manual pages that say the rest.

## The Mouse

Plan 9 expects a mouse with three buttons, and Acme gives each a job. Button 1
selects text. Button 2 runs text as a command. Button 3 looks at text: it opens
the file the text names, or searches for the next place the text occurs. On a
Mac, Option-click is button 2 and Command-click is button 3.

On the background, button 3 opens the menu of rio. Choose *New*, then sweep out
the new window with button 3. To resize a window, drag its border with button
1; to move it, drag the border with button 3.

## Windows in Acme

Acme divides its screen into columns, and each column into windows. A window
is two pieces of text: a tag, the line at its top, and a body below it. The tag
holds the name of the window, usually a file name, and the commands that apply
to it; the body holds the file. Both are text, and you can edit the tag as
freely as the body, adding a command to it or changing the name.

The bar at the left of a body scrolls it. Button 1 in the bar moves back
through the text and button 3 moves forward, further the lower in the bar you
click; button 2 jumps to that point in the file. The scroll wheel works as well.

## Commands

In Acme, any text can be a command: type it anywhere and click it with button
2. Try

	ls /bin
	echo hello

The words in a tag are commands like any other. *Put* saves the file, *Undo*
takes back the last change, *Get* reads the file again, and *Del* closes the
window. *New* makes an empty window: type a file name in its tag, and *Put*
makes the file.

To open a file, type its name anywhere and click it with button 3. A manual
page works the same way: button 3 on acme(1) shows that page. In both cases
the text goes to the plumber(4), which decides from rules in /sys/lib/plumb
what the text is and which program should have it. Most of what follows works
the same way.

One window runs win, which runs rc(1), the Plan 9 shell, in an Acme window. Its
body ends in the rc prompt, %. Type a command
at its end and press Return; ^C interrupts it.

## Keys

Typing goes to the window under the mouse, wherever the mouse rests. These keys
act on that window:

| Key | Does |
| --- | ---- |
| Cmd-S | Put: save the file |
| Cmd-A | select the whole window |
| Cmd-P | find a file or window by name, in a Search window |
| Cmd-Shift-F | search the text of the files, in a Search window |
| Ctl-X Ctl-S, Ctl-X K | Put and Del, as in Emacs |

The keys are rules read by hotkey(1). Your lib/hotkey/acme includes
/lib/hotkey/acme.emacs, which includes /lib/hotkey/acme. Add your own below the
include line; a key you bind replaces the system binding for it. The first line
of the file, a comment, gives the command that applies your changes: select the
command after the # with button 1, then click it with button 2.

## Appearance

The font is $font, set in your lib/profile. fontfs(4) serves Plan 9 fonts at
any size, made from the scalable fonts in /lib/font/ttf, under /mnt/font.
Programs take their colors from a theme, a file of names and colors, read from
your lib/theme, or /lib/theme in its place; see theme(6). hl(1) colors the
text of each window by its syntax.

## Files

When you drop a file on the page, it lands in the drop directory here and goes
to the plumber, as if you had clicked its name with button 3. The plumber goes
by what the file holds, as file(1) reports it, so any name will do. Text opens
in Acme, an image or a PDF in page(1), and a Word or RTF document in Acme as
its text.

Sound plays in deck(1): MP3, M4A, Ogg, FLAC and WAV files. Deck is a window with
a scrub bar, and each sound dropped after the first joins its playlist. Space
pauses it, and *Exit* on its button 3 menu stops it. The browser plays sound
once you have clicked the page.

A zip opens as a directory. zipopen mounts it with fs/zipfs (see tapefs(4)) at
/n/zip, named as the zip file, less .zip, and opens that directory in Acme,
where its files open and play as any others.

A URL opens in a new tab of the browser, by button 3 as a file does. Where the
browser blocks the tab, a bar at the top of the page shows the link.

To save a file to your computer,

	download file

puts each file named in the downloads of the browser. Chrome can also lend the
system a folder of your computer, which stays across visits:

	local add $home/src

asks which folder, then binds it at $home/src. Safari keeps its folders to
itself, so local works in Chrome alone. See local(3).

## Programs and Packages

A package is a tree of files added to the name space in place, so a window can
have one version of a package and the next window another. Your profile adds
the packages in your lib/pkg at each start; to add one in a window,

	pkg add ssh

and see pkg(1). Your lib/pkg starts with git9, the git from 9front, written for
Plan 9; git(1) describes its commands, such as git/clone. The git package is
the git from git-scm.com, for the commands git9 leaves out. diff(1) -u writes
unified diffs, and patch(1) applies them. Programs written for Unix run
through APE, the ANSI/POSIX environment: Python is one.

## Other Machines

The ssh package is ssh, scp and sftp from OpenSSH. Programs that need a
terminal run in vt(1), an xterm:

	vt ssh devbox

sshfs(4) mounts the files of another machine by sftp. cpu(1) runs a command,
or a shell, on a Linux machine named in your lib/cpu, which sees this name
space as a Plan 9 cpu server sees the name space of its terminal.

The machine this page came from serves its files to import(4), and a passkey
signs you in to it. A comment in your lib/profile gives the commands that do
it, for you to try.

## Language Servers

A language server knows a language: the definition of a name, its uses,
the errors in a file. lspfs(4) runs servers and serves what they
know as files, and alsp(4) answers the keys in Acme from them. Rules in
/lib/lsp, or in your lib/lsp in its place, say which server serves which
files. With a server for the language of the file, these keys work:

| Key | Does |
| --- | ---- |
| Cmd-B or F12 | Def: go to the definition of the word at the cursor |
| Cmd-Shift-B | Refs: list its uses |
| Cmd-I | Doc: show its documentation, which follows the cursor |
| Cmd-. | Act: fixes from the language server, then 1–9 picks, Esc cancels |
| F2 | Rename: type the new name after it in the tag, then Esc and button 2 |
| Cmd-Shift-O | Syms: list the functions and types of the file |
| Cmd-K, then R, I, T, D, F or C | Refs, Impls, Type, Diag, Fmt, Comp |

## Drawn Windows

Each of these commands, run in Acme, opens a window that draws its file, which
stays text that Acme can edit:

	Sketch file.pic    a drawing held by constraints, after Sketchpad; sketch(1)
	Dag file.dot       a graph in the dot language, laid out by dot; dag(1)
	Grap file          a graph of data, whose points can be moved; grapw(1)
	Chess file.pgn     a game of chess, with an engine to play; chess(1)

Tree, in the tag of a window, opens an outline of the parse of its text; see
Tree(1). sticky(1) keeps notes, each a small colored window of Markdown,
where you left it.

## Writing

Huh, in the tag of a window, colors the text by its surprisal, the bits a
small language model spends on each word, so the words that carry the most
information stand out. Bits colors every token by its bits. See huh(1).
adict(1) looks words up in the dictionaries of dict(7); button 3 on a word in
an entry looks that word up.

## Searching and Watching

Search opens a window whose first line is a query; the lines below it are what
matches, best first, changing as you type. Cmd-P and Cmd-Shift-F open it.
Watch runs a command, mk by default, each time Acme writes a file in its
directory, and shows the output, so a Put shows what the change did. See
Search(1) and Watch(1).

## Sibyl

sibyl(1) is a coding agent that works in Acme. It needs a key for the model in
factotum(4), with proto=pass and service=anthropic or service=opencode, as
sibylfs(4) says. Keys in your lib/factotum go to factotum at each start.

Select some text and run `|sibyl` with an instruction, such as

	|sibyl add error checks

The answer replaces the selection, and *Undo* takes it back. `win sibyl -i`
holds a conversation instead. Its changes go to a copy of the project, and
*Review* in its tag shows them as hunks to apply or drop. *Sibyl* lists your
saved sessions. With text selected, Cmd-K then E types an instruction for it
in the tag, and Cmd-K then S puts the selected lines in the session, for a
question.
!

. lib/profile
